  "response": "Localisation of acute weakness begins at the bedside, where the distribution of the deficit and its accompanying symptoms are catalogued before any instrument is applied. Myopathic weakness predominantly affects proximal limb-girdle muscles such as the shoulders and hips; neuropathic weakness, by contrast, is frequently accompanied by sensory symptoms such as numbness or tingling. The laboratory stage of the assessment then rests on two techniques with quite different targets. Nerve conduction studies measure the conduction velocity and response amplitude of peripheral nerves, whereas needle electromyography records electrical activity arising within muscle fibres.\n\nInterpreting the conduction data demands care about what a slow velocity actually signifies. Demyelinating neuropathies produce marked slowing of nerve conduction velocity. Nerve conduction velocity is markedly slowed in primary muscle disease; this cannot be squared with the attribution of marked slowing to loss of the myelin sheath along the nerve trunk, and the two accounts of the origin of slowing cannot both be true. The resolution of that tension governs how any velocity figure is reported in a weakness workup.\n\nThe needle examination of the weak limb supplied the decisive waveforms in the case under discussion. Electromyography recorded small, short-duration motor unit potentials. Motor unit action potentials in myopathy are characteristically large in amplitude and long in duration, a characterisation that the small, short potentials obtained at the needle rule out, since a single myopathic process cannot generate both profiles at once. In Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome, cerebrospinal fluid analysis shows raised protein with a normal cell count, and that albuminocytological pattern in the sampled fluid confirmed the treating neurologist's attribution of the patient's sudden weakness to peripheral nerve disease. Subsequently, the consulting neuromuscular specialist attributed the patient's sudden weakness to primary muscle disease, and the small, short-duration potentials on needle examination stand as the evidence for that attribution, indicating a fault within the muscle fibre itself.\n\nSettling the disagreement fell to tissue rather than to waveform. Either the muscle biopsy specimen contained no inflammatory cell infiltrate, or the muscle biopsy specimen contained an inflammatory cell infiltrate: exactly one of these two states of the sampled tissue holds, and there is no third option. The specialist panel's final report determined that the muscle biopsy showed an inflammatory cell infiltrate \u2014 that is, the report asserts the second of those two possibilities, the presence of inflammatory cells within the specimen. A disputed localisation is in this way closed not by argument about which clinical pattern looks more typical, but by adjudicated review of the histology, whose verdict fixes the final diagnosis and the treatment that follows from it.",
